<markdown_cell>
# Bats-core test suite for production Bash scripts: health check, log rotation, and secret rotation

This notebook presents three bats-core test suites that verify the behavior of production Bash scripts in critical operational areas: service health checks, log rotation, and secret rotation. Each suite follows the bats-core conventions of per-test isolation, setup/teardown hooks, and assertion helpers.

> **Scope** — bats-core v1.14.0, Bash 5.3.20 (patchlevel 2026-09-15). ShellCheck v0.11.0 used as a lint gate.
</markdown_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## Purpose

Production Bash scripts must be tested the same way other software is tested — deterministically, in isolation, and on every change. bats-core (Bash Automated Testing System) provides a minimal framework for writing `.bats` test files that run individual test cases with isolated subshells, shared setup/teardown lifecycle hooks, and assertion helpers.

This notebook covers three domains where Bash scripts are most commonly deployed to production:

- **Health checks** — scripts that probe whether a service is responding correctly.
- **Log rotation** — scripts that archive, compress, and prune log files.
- **Secret rotation** — scripts that regenerate credentials and redistribute them to consumers.

Each suite is structured so it can run in CI via `bats tests/` and can be extended with stub executables on a fake `PATH` prefix so tests never contact real services.
</markdown_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## When to use

| Scenario | Use this suite |
|---|---|
| CI pipeline runs on every PR | Run all three suites via `bats tests/` |
| Adding a new health-check probe | Add a test to `health-check.bats` |
| Changing rotation retention policy | Add a test to `log-rotation.bats` |
| Modifying secret regeneration logic | Add a test to `secret-rotation.bats` |
| Testing a script that hits external services | Prefix `PATH` with stub directory in setup |
</markdown_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## Prerequisites

- **bats-core v1.14.0** — `brew install bats-core` or download from the [bats-core releases](https://github.com/bats-core/bats-core/releases/tag/v1.14.0). Run tests with `bats tests/`.
- **Bash 4.3+** — the test scripts use arrays and `set -o pipefail`; Bash 5.3+ recommended.
- **ShellCheck v0.11.0** — lint gate: `shellcheck -x -S error` on each script under test.
- **`bats` on `PATH`** — or invoke via `$(brew --prefix bats-core)/bin/bats`.
</markdown_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## Test suite structure

```
tests/
├── health-check.bats
├── log-rotation.bats
├── secret-rotation.bats
└── setup.bash
```

The `setup.bash` file is loaded by every test file via `load setup` (bats-core built-in). It defines the fake `PATH` prefix and stub executables used across all suites.
</markdown_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## Suite 1: Health check tests

Tests that a health-check script correctly detects healthy services, unhealthy services, and partially degraded multi-component deployments.
</markdown_cell>

<code_cell>
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# tests/health-check.bats
# bats-core test suite for service health-check scripts.
# Stubs: curl, nc — provided by tests/setup.bash

load setup

@test "healthy service returns 0 and reports OK" {
  stub curl --output "$STUBDIR/healthy.json" <<'EOF'
{"status":"ok"}
EOF
  stub nc -r0

  run ./health-check.sh --url http://localhost:8080/health
  [ "$status" -eq 0 ]
  [ "${lines[0]}" == "OK" ]
}

@test "unhealthy service returns non-zero and reports CRITICAL" {
  stub curl --output "$STUBDIR/unhealthy.json" <<'EOF'
{"status":"degraded"}
EOF
  stub nc -r1

  run ./health-check.sh --url http://localhost:8080/health
  [ "$status" -eq 1 ]
  [ "${lines[0]}" == "CRITICAL" ]
}

@test "missing service times out and reports TIMEOUT" {
  stub curl --return-error-code=28

  run ./health-check.sh --url http://localhost:8080/health --timeout 2
  [ "$status" -eq 2 ]
  [ "${lines[0]}" == "TIMEOUT" ]
}
</code_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## Suite 2: Log rotation tests

Tests that a log-rotation script rotates files above a size threshold, compresses archives with gzip, prunes archives beyond retention, and signals the application to reopen log handles.
</markdown_cell>

<code_cell>
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# tests/log-rotation.bats
# bats-core test suite for log-rotation scripts.
# Stubs: gzip, find, stat, touch, chown — provided by tests/setup.bash

load setup

setup_file() {
  mkdir -p "$BATS_TEST_TMPDIR/logs"
  LOG_DIR="$BATS_TEST_TMPDIR/logs"
  export LOG_DIR
}

@test "rotates a log file exceeding the size threshold" {
  stub gzip -r0
  stub stat --printf="%s" -r10485761
  stub touch -r0
  stub chown -r0
  stub find -r0

  run ./log-rotate.sh --dir "$LOG_DIR" --max-bytes 10485760
  [ "$status" -eq 0 ]
  [ -f "$LOG_DIR/app-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S).log.gz" ]
}

@test "does not rotate a log below the size threshold" {
  stub stat --printf="%s" -r5242880
  stub gzip -r1
  stub find -r1

  run ./log-rotate.sh --dir "$LOG_DIR" --max-bytes 10485760
  [ "$status" -eq 0 ]
  [ ! -f "$LOG_DIR/"*.log.gz ]
}

@test "prunes archives older than retention period" {
  stub find -r0

  # Create fake archives older than retention
  touch -t 202608010000 "$LOG_DIR/app-20260801.log.gz"
  touch -t 202609100000 "$LOG_DIR/app-20260910.log.gz"

  run ./log-rotate.sh --dir "$LOG_DIR" --max-bytes 10485760 --retention-days 7
  [ "$status" -eq 0 ]
  [ ! -f "$LOG_DIR/app-20260801.log.gz" ]
}
</code_cell>

<markdown_cell>
## Suite 3: Secret rotation tests

Tests that a secret-rotation script generates new credentials, updates the target service configuration, verifies the new credentials work, and invalidates the old ones.
</markdown_cell>

<code_cell>
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# tests/secret-rotation.bats
# bats-core test suite for secret-rotation scripts.
# Stubs: openssl, systemctl, curl, kubectl — provided by tests/setup.bash

load setup

@test "generates new credentials and updates config" {
  stub openssl -r0 <<'EOF'
NewSecretValue:abc123def456
EOF
  stub systemctl -r0
  stub curl -r0

  run ./secret-rotate.sh --service myapp --config /etc/myapp/config.yaml
  [ "$status" -eq 0 ]
  [ "${lines[0]}" == "Rotation complete" ]
}

@test "rolls back on verification failure" {
  stub openssl -r0 <<'EOF'
NewSecretValue:abc123def456
EOF
  stub systemctl -r0
  stub curl --return-error-code=5

  run ./secret-rotate.sh --service myapp --config /etc/myapp/config.yaml
  [ "$status" -eq 1 ]
  [ "${lines[0]}" == "Verification failed; rolled back" ]
}

@test "invalidates old secret after successful rotation" {
  stub openssl -r0
  stub systemctl -r0
  stub curl -r0
  stub kubectl -r0

  run ./secret-rotate.sh --service myapp --config /etc/myapp/config.yaml --invalidate-old
  [ "$status" -eq 0 ]
  [ "${lines[-1]}" == "Old secret invalidated" ]
}
</code_cell>

